permalink: /solution/index.html
title: The solution
description: >-
  PATON is a smart nurse-call system: a device at the bed raises a specific need or an
  emergency, and nurse devices receive it as a prioritised, routed alert.
---
<section class="page-head">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">The solution</p>
    <h1>Same button. Far better call.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      PATON is a nurse-call system that carries meaning. A patient still reaches the ward
      instantly — that part is untouchable — but the ward now receives a request it can act on
      before anyone starts walking.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      <article class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-top: 0">At the bed</p>
        <h3>The bed device</h3>
        <p>
          Instead of a single anonymous button, the patient chooses from a short list of the things
          patients actually ask for. Alongside it sits one unmistakable emergency action that always
          goes out at the highest priority, no menu in the way.
        </p>
        <ul style="margin-top: 1.25rem; color: var(--ink-2)">
          <li>A specific need, not a generic alert</li>
          <li>A separate emergency action that never queues behind anything</li>
          <li>Deliberately few choices, so it stays usable when someone is unwell</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-top: 0">On shift</p>
        <h3>The nurse device</h3>
        <p>
          Staff on duty carry a device that lists the open requests for their ward. Each alert
          carries the room and bed, what is being asked for, how long it has been waiting, and the
          qualification the request calls for.
        </p>
        <ul style="margin-top: 1.25rem; color: var(--ink-2)">
          <li>Emergencies first, then by how long a request has waited</li>
          <li>Requests matched to the qualification that can resolve them</li>
          <li>Enough information to arrive with the right thing in hand</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--soft">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The loop</p>
      <h2>From press to resolved</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Raised</h3>
        <p>The patient picks a need, or triggers an emergency. Both leave the bed immediately.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Prioritised</h3>
        <p>
          The request is ordered against everything else open on the ward, with emergencies always
          at the top.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Routed</h3>
        <p>
          It reaches the nurse devices on shift, tagged with the qualification the request needs so
          it lands with someone who can finish it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Recorded</h3>
        <p>
          Type, time and outcome are logged. Over a few weeks that becomes an honest picture of what
          a ward is actually being asked for.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="shell split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">What changes</p>
      <h2>Fewer wasted walks, fewer wrong-person visits, fewer pointless interruptions.</h2>
      <p>
        None of this is exotic. It is the difference between “someone in 209 needs something” and
        “bed 1 in 209 needs pain relief and has been waiting a minute and a half” — and that
        difference is where a ward's lost minutes live.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also means that, for the first time, the ward has data about its own call load rather
        than an impression of it.
      </p>
      <div class="button-row" style="margin-top: 1.75rem">
        <a class="button" href="/case-study/">See it running on a real ward</a>
        <a class="button button--ghost" href="/savings-calculator/">What is that worth?</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>What we are not claiming</h3>
      <p>
        PATON does not diagnose anything, does not decide anything clinical, and does not replace
        anyone's judgement. It routes requests and it keeps a record. Everything on this site that
        is a model rather than a measurement is labelled as one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
